<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/ratios-percentages-proportion">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Counting tells you <em>how many</em>; comparison tells you what the number means. A
    stadium holding 60,000 sounds enormous — until you learn the city holds six million. The
    mathematics of comparison comes in three connected forms. A <strong>ratio</strong> is a
    comparison frozen into a number: 3:2 flour to water, one chef to five tables. A
    <strong>percentage</strong> is just a ratio out of 100 — "83%" literally means 83 per
    cent, 83 per hundred — chosen as a common yardstick so comparisons become easy. And
    <strong>proportion</strong> is the assumption that a ratio stays fixed as quantities
    change: double the flour, double the water; twice the distance, twice the fuel. Nearly
    every headline statistic, shop discount, recipe, map scale and exchange rate is one of
    these three — and, as we will see, most statistical lies are one of them misused.
  </p>
  <p>
    To handle them well you also need to know what kind of number you are comparing with. So
    this lesson first surveys the number zoo itself — the different species of numbers, and
    the set language mathematicians use to organise them — then builds up powers and standard
    form for handling very large and very small comparisons, and finally develops ratios,
    percentages and proportional reasoning, ending with the most dramatic proportional
    process of all: exponential growth, the mathematics of doubling.
  </p>

  <h2>The number zoo, and the sets that organise it</h2>
  <p>
    Numbers come in nested species. The <strong>natural numbers</strong> 1, 2, 3, … are the
    counting numbers; add zero and their negatives and you get the <strong>integers</strong>.
    Ratios of integers — ½, −3, 0.75, any terminating or repeating decimal — are the
    <strong>rational numbers</strong>. But the ancient Greeks discovered to their shock that
    the diagonal of a unit square, √2, is <em>not</em> a ratio of any integers: its decimal
    runs forever without repeating. Such numbers are <strong>irrational</strong> (√2, π, e),
    and rationals plus irrationals together form the <strong>real numbers</strong> — the
    complete number line. A <em>common misconception</em>: the number line is "full" of
    decimals because there are infinitely many fractions between any two of them. True, yet
    the irrationals are <em>more</em> numerous in a precise sense (Cantor proved it in 1874):
    if you threw a dart at the number line, it would hit an irrational with probability 1.
    The fractions, infinite as they are, are a dust.
  </p>
  <p>
    This nesting is handled with <strong>set</strong> language: a set is a collection with a
    definite membership (ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℚ ⊂ ℝ), drawn as nested circles on a Venn diagram.
    <strong>Intersection</strong> (∩) means "in both", <strong>union</strong> (∪) "in either",
    and the complement is "outside". The vocabulary sounds formal but earns its keep the
    moment you meet overlapping categories in the wild — "students taking physics ∪ chemistry"
    or the events that can and cannot happen together in probability, later in this ladder.
  </p>

  <h2>Powers, roots and indices: shorthand for repeated multiplication</h2>
  <p>
    Powers exist because repeated multiplication is as common as repeated addition. 2⁵ means
    2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2, and three index laws do all the algebra: multiplying powers
    <strong>adds</strong> indices (2³ × 2² = 2⁵), dividing <strong>subtracts</strong> them
    (2⁵ ÷ 2² = 2³), and a power of a power <strong>multiplies</strong> them ((2²)³ = 2⁶) —
    each law is just counting the multiplications. The definitions that fall out are
    beautiful: the zero index a⁰ = 1 (so that dividing a³ by a³ gives a⁰ = 1), negative
    indices as reciprocals (2⁻¹ = ½), and fractional indices as roots: 2^(1/2) = √2, because
    squaring it returns 2. Roots are the inverse operation — √81 = 9 asks "9 × what = 81" —
    and squares and square roots are pairs, like packing and unpacking. <em>Common
    misconception:</em> a negative index makes a negative number. It does not: 2⁻³ = 1/8, a
    small positive number — the minus sign sends you below 1, never below 0.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard form: taming the astronomical</h2>
  <p>
    Science refuses to write 0.000000000000000000000021 g or 300,000,000 m/s by hand, and
    <strong>standard form</strong> (scientific notation) is the fix: every number written as
    A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A &lt; 10. The speed of light is 3 × 10⁸ m/s; the mass of a hydrogen atom
    is 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. The notation is really applied index laws: multiplying
    (3 × 10⁸) × (2 × 10⁻¹⁰) multiplies the As and <em>adds</em> the indices to give
    6 × 10⁻². Worked example: a virus at 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m and a red blood cell at 7.5 × 10⁻⁶ m —
    the ratio is (7.5 ÷ 1.2) × 10⁻⁶⁻⁽⁻⁷⁾ = 6.25 × 10¹ = 62.5, so about 63 viruses would span
    a blood cell. Without the notation, that comparison is an error factory.
  </p>

  <h2>Ratio and proportion: the mathematics of recipes</h2>
  <p>
    A ratio like 3 : 2 is a <em>statement of structure</em>: for every 3 parts of one thing,
    2 of another. Its power comes from the <strong>unitary method</strong> — find one part,
    scale to any total. Concrete case: concrete mix 1 : 2 : 3 (cement : sand : gravel) for
    240 kg total means 6 parts, so one part is 40 kg — 40, 80, 120 kg respectively. Change
    the total and the ratios never move; that is <strong>direct proportion</strong>, written
    y ∝ x, and it is the mathematics behind currency exchange, map scales (1 : 50,000 means
    1 cm on the map is 50,000 cm in the field), and the price of anything sold by weight.
    <strong>Inverse proportion</strong> is its mirror: more workers, fewer days — 6 builders
    finish in 12 days, so 9 builders take 6 × 12 ÷ 9 = 8 days, because what stays fixed is
    the <em>product</em> (36 builder-days of work), not the ratio. Mixing these up —
    assuming more workers means longer, or that halving the recipe halves the cooking time —
    is the classic trap. The honest test is always: <em>what quantity is actually constant?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Ratios, fractions, decimals and percentages are one language in four alphabets, and
    fluency means translating instantly: 3 : 5 as a share of a whole is 3/8 and 5/8 of it,
    or 37.5% and 62.5%. A worked share: prize money of £600 split 2 : 3 : 5 has 10 parts,
    so one part is £60 — £120, £180 and £300, whose sum checks back to £600. Simplifying
    uses the same move as simplifying fractions — divide by common factors (12 : 18 = 2 : 3)
    — and comparing ratios needs a common second term (3 : 4 versus 5 : 7 becomes 21 : 28
    versus 20 : 28, so 3 : 4 is the steeper ratio). One warning from the kitchen: a ratio
    comparison only makes sense between quantities of the same kind — 3 eggs to 2 cups of
    flour is a recipe, not a ratio to be scaled against 3 eggs to 2 cups of sugar, and
    mixing "parts of" different totals is the commonest source of ratio errors in exams
    and in real catering alike.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="percentage change, computed honestly — drag both values and watch the baseline do the work" widget="sliderLab" config={pctCfg} />

  <h2>Percentages: ratios with a shared yardstick</h2>
  <p>
    Percentages are ratios normalised to 100, and every percentage calculation reduces to one
    move: the <strong>multiplier</strong>. A 20% increase is ×1.20; a 20% discount is ×0.80;
    two successive changes <em>multiply</em> (up 10% then down 10% is ×1.1 × 0.9 = ×0.99 — a
    net 1% <em>loss</em>, because the second 10% is taken of a bigger base). As the widget
    shows, <strong>percentage change always divides by the old value</strong>, so the answer
    depends on which direction you travel: a rise from 100 to 150 is +50%, but the fall from
    150 back to 100 is −33%. "Up 50%, down 50%" does not return to the start — percentages
    compound, they do not add. The <strong>reverse percentage</strong> problem — a coat costs
    £84 after a 30% discount, what was the original? — is solved by running the multiplier
    backwards: 84 ÷ 0.70 = £120, <em>not</em> 84 + 30% of 84 = £109.20. Finding the original
    base is the single most-asked percentage question in shops, tax and pay rises alike.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "percentage points and per cent are the same." If a bank
    rate rises from 2% to 3%, that is one <strong>percentage point</strong> — but a
    <strong>50% increase</strong> in the rate. News reports swap these constantly, and the
    difference between "unemployment fell 2%" and "fell 2 percentage points" can be the whole
    story. Every percentage needs a named baseline: "50% faster" than what? A percentage
    without a stated base is a rumour.
  </p>

  <h2>Exponential growth: proportion taken to the limit</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest proportional process is the one where the <em>ratio per step</em> is constant:
    each period multiplies by the same factor. That is <strong>exponential growth</strong> —
    money at 7% a year becomes A = P × 1.07ⁿ after n years; a population of 1,000 bacteria
    doubling every 20 minutes becomes 1,000 × 2ⁿ. The signature of exponential change is that
    <strong>equal ratios in equal times</strong>, so the graph curves upward ever more
    steeply, and the useful intuition is <strong>doubling time</strong>: the "rule of 72"
    says money at r% interest doubles in roughly 72/r years — 7.2% a year, about a decade.
    The mirror process, <strong>exponential decay</strong>, multiplies by a constant factor
    below 1: a drug's concentration halving every 4 hours, carbon-14 decaying by half every
    ~5,730 years — radiocarbon dating is reverse percentage change with a very old baseline.
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "exponential" means "very fast". It means
    <em>constant-ratio</em>: it starts slow — a doubling from 1 to 2 looks trivial — and the
    scary part is that thirty doublings later the number is a billion. The lily-pond that
    fills on day 30 was half empty on day 29.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer connects the topics you have just met into one mechanism. Exponential
    growth is where indices, ratios and percentages fuse: "grows by 7% per year" <em>is</em>
    the index law A = P × 1.07ⁿ, and undoing it — "how long until it doubles?" — is a
    logarithm question (n = log 2 ÷ log 1.07 ≈ 10.2), the logarithm being precisely the
    inverse of the exponential. This is why a slide rule, a pH scale and an earthquake
    magnitude are all logarithms in disguise: they exist to turn <em>multiplicative</em>
    comparisons into <em>additive</em> ones, the same translation you already perform every
    time you write 10⁶ instead of 1,000,000.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two nuances separate the fluent from the merely trained. First,
    <strong>compounding frequency matters</strong>: £1,000 at 12% once a year becomes £1,120,
    but at 1% a month it becomes 1,000 × 1.01¹² ≈ £1,126.8 — and as compounding gets
    continuous the multiplier approaches e ≈ 2.71828, the natural base that calculus will
    later show to be the special rate where growth equals the current amount. Second,
    <strong>proportional reasoning is the check, not just the method</strong>: before any
    computation, ask what the answer should do when an input doubles. Fuel cost should double
    with distance (direct), time with the number of pages (direct), but not stopping distance
    with speed (it grows as the square — the physics later in this ladder). Innumeracy, as
    the mathematician John Allen Paulos put it, is mostly the failure to run these quick
    proportionality sanity checks; numeracy is having them on reflex.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Prealgebra &amp; Elementary Algebra; Paulos, Innumeracy; Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E1).</p>
</TopicPage>
